  "title": "Selling the harbour: land, risk and the entrepreneurial city in a mid-sized Dutch municipality",
  "perspective": "Marxist political economy: Harvey's (1989) shift from urban managerialism to entrepreneurialism, with entrepreneurial public land policy (Hyötyläinen and Haila 2018), financialisation (Savini and Aalbers 2016) and the capitalist state (Foglesong 2003). Week 6.",
  "text": [
   "Constructed practice text, written for this site in the style of an academic article; not a real publication. The city, the actors, the figures and all quotations from officials and residents are hypothetical. Only the cited authors are real course readings.",
   "Abstract. This article examines the redevelopment of a former shipyard area in a mid-sized Dutch city between 2012 and 2024. It argues that the project cannot be understood as the outcome of local planning choices alone. The transformation of the harbour was shaped by the pressures that a competitive urban system places on municipalities that depend on land revenues, and by the growing role of investment capital in the production of the built environment. Drawing on policy documents and interviews with officials, the article shows how the municipality moved from providing housing and services for its residents to speculating on the future value of a single place, and how the costs and benefits of that shift were unevenly distributed.",
   "Introduction. For much of the post-war period, Dutch municipalities practised what is often called active land policy: they bought land, serviced it and sold building plots, using the proceeds to fund public facilities and social housing. In this arrangement the municipality acted mainly as a provider, and the aim was to improve conditions across the municipal territory as a whole. After the financial crisis of 2008, many municipalities faced losses on their land holdings, and the city studied here was no exception. Its response is the subject of this article.",
   "I read the case through a long-running debate on the changing character of urban governance. Harvey (1989) described a shift from managerialism to entrepreneurialism, in which cities increasingly behave like firms competing for investment, consumers and mobile capital. Later work has shown how public landowners adopt the same logic. In Helsinki, Hyötyläinen and Haila (2018) describe an entrepreneurial public real estate policy through which the city sold land to maximise revenue. In Milan, Savini and Aalbers (2016) show how land that is turned into a financial asset becomes detached from the local context in which it is planned. These studies suggest that planning outcomes are not simply decided in the council chamber; they are conditioned by the wider dynamics of accumulation in which cities are embedded.",
   "The case. The shipyard closed in the 1990s, leaving 40 hectares of contaminated land owned largely by the municipality. A first plan from 2006 proposed 1,800 homes, of which 30 per cent would be social rented housing, together with a school and a public park along the water. After 2008 the municipal land company reported large write-downs, and the council looked for a partner that could carry the risk of development. In 2014 the city signed a joint venture agreement with a consortium of an institutional investor and two developers.",
   "The agreement changed the project in three ways. First, the programme was revised to attract 'high-value residents and international visitors': the share of social housing was reduced to 15 per cent, and a 120-metre residential tower and a waterfront hotel were added as icons for the marketing of the city. Second, the municipality kept responsibility for soil remediation and the quay walls, while the consortium acquired the right to develop the most profitable plots first. As one official put it, \"the city took the ground risk; the market took the view\". Third, the land price was calculated from the expected yield of the completed rental blocks, so that the residual land value depended on the rent levels the investor needed to achieve. Once this calculation was fixed, the social programme became the variable that had to give way whenever costs rose.",
   "Analysis. The harbour illustrates how the municipality's role shifted from managing a territory to constructing a place. The revised plan did little for the existing neighbourhoods east of the site, where waiting lists for social housing grew, but a great deal for the image of the city in a regional competition for higher-income households and investors. Officials consistently justified the changes by reference to other cities: \"If we do not build a skyline, other cities will take the investors\", a council report argued. This competition did not operate as a neutral market test of good ideas. It worked as a discipline that narrowed what the city believed it could afford to demand.",
   "The case also shows that the risks of the project were socialised while its gains were privatised. The municipality absorbed the costs of remediation and infrastructure, while the uplift in land value created by these public works was largely captured in the investor's valuation. In this sense the local state acted less as a regulator of development than as a facilitator of capital investment in the built environment. When construction costs rose sharply in 2022, the consortium requested and obtained a further reduction of social housing; the municipality, with millions already sunk in the quay walls, had little bargaining power left.",
   "Finally, the case reveals a recurring tension. The value of the harbour land is produced collectively, by public investment, by the growth of the city and by the work of earlier generations on the site, yet it is privately appropriated. Planning is needed to coordinate development, but it is constrained from capturing this collectively produced value whenever doing so would threaten the returns on which private investment depends (Foglesong, 2003).",
   "Conclusion. The redevelopment of the harbour should not be read as a story of poor local decisions or weak negotiators. The municipality's room for manoeuvre was set by structural pressures: fiscal dependence on land revenues, inter-urban competition and the growing weight of investment capital. Planning in the city did not disappear; it was redirected towards making a place attractive to capital. As long as these conditions persist, more skilful planners alone are unlikely to produce different outcomes. Any alternative requires changes in how land value is owned and distributed, and in the wider pressures that set cities against one another."
  ],
